{"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swift"}],"role":"article","title":"Customizing Your C Code for Swift","roleHeading":"Article"},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Language-Interoperability-with-C-and-C++","identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift"],"generated":true,"title":"Language Interoperability with C and C++"}],"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"kind":"article","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Because structures in C can’t have methods, property accessors, or custom initializers,"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"you often need to write such functionality using global functions. Structures in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Swift can declare methods, property accessors, and initializers. You use the "},{"code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"macro to group together related global functions into a single structure type that’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"imported into Swift."}]},{"anchor":"Apply-CFSWIFTNAME-to-Related-Functions","text":"Apply CF_SWIFT_NAME to Related Functions","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The example below shows several functions that are all related to a "},{"code":"Color","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro is applied to each function, giving each one a new name"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"for Swift that’s nested together under the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Color"},{"type":"text","text":" type:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"occ","code":["Color ColorCreateWithCMYK(float c, float m, float y, float k) CF_SWIFT_NAME(Color.init(c:m:y:k:));","","float ColorGetHue(Color color) CF_SWIFT_NAME(getter:Color.hue(self:));","void ColorSetHue(Color color, float hue) CF_SWIFT_NAME(setter:Color.hue(self:newValue:));","","Color ColorDarkenColor(Color color, float amount) CF_SWIFT_NAME(Color.darken(self:amount:));","","extern const Color ColorBondiBlue CF_SWIFT_NAME(Color.bondiBlue);","","Color ColorGetCalibrationColor(void) CF_SWIFT_NAME(getter:Color.calibration());","Color ColorSetCalibrationColor(Color color) CF_SWIFT_NAME(setter:Color.calibration(newValue:));"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The argument you pass to the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME"},{"type":"text","text":" macro uses the same syntax as the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#selector"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"expression. You use "},{"code":"self","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in a "},{"code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" argument to refer to the instance"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that the method belongs to.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Import-Related-Functions-into-Swift","type":"heading","text":"Import Related Functions into Swift","level":3},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Here’s how Swift imports the related functions above into a single type:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["extension Color {","    init(c: Float, m: Float, y: Float, k: Float)","","    var hue: Float { get set }","","    func darken(amount: Float) -> Color","","    static var bondiBlue: Color","","    static var calibration: Color","}"]},{"name":"Note","type":"aside","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You can’t reorder or change the number of arguments for type members imported"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"using the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME"},{"type":"text","text":" macro."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"note"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"abstract":[{"text":"Use the ","type":"text"},{"code":"CF_SWIFT_NAME","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro to group functions that have related behavior."}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","kind":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Learn how Swift represents imported C structures and unions, including types with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"bitfields and unnamed fields.","type":"text"}],"title":"Using Imported C Structs and Unions in Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","role":"article"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift":{"abstract":[{"text":"Learn how to call imported functions that are declared in a C header.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift","title":"Using Imported C Functions in Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift","role":"article"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift":{"role":"collection","abstract":[{"text":"Build apps using a powerful open language.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/swift","title":"Swift","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift","type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift":{"type":"topic","role":"article","kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"Use imported C-defined macros as constants.","type":"text"}],"title":"Using Imported C Macros in Swift","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift"}}}